---
import NarrativePage from "@/components/authority/NarrativePage.astro";
import { createFrameworkPillarJsonLd } from "@/lib/seo/jsonLd";

const pageTitle = "Power User Trap℠ by Lozen Advisory";
const seoTitle = "Power User Trap℠ by Lozen Advisory | Hidden AI Verification Labor";
const description =
  "Lozen Advisory’s Power User Trap℠ framework identifies when organizations relocate hidden verification, correction, complexity, or system-repair work onto high-capacity employees.";
const canonicalUrl = "https://www.lozenadvisory.com/frameworks/power-user-trap/";
const termDescription =
  "Lozen Advisory’s framework for identifying when organizations relocate hidden verification, correction, complexity, or system-repair work onto high-capacity employees without formally assigning authority, capacity, evidence, or refusal rights.";

const articleJsonLd = createFrameworkPillarJsonLd({
  title: pageTitle,
  description,
  href: "/frameworks/power-user-trap/",
  termName: "Power User Trap℠",
  termDescription,
  termSetName: "Disclosure-Independent Governance by Lozen Advisory",
  termSetDescription:
    "Lozen Advisory’s category framework and proprietary vocabulary for evaluating risks legacy systems were not built to see.",
  termSetHref: "/disclosure-independent-governance/",
  breadcrumbs: [
    { name: "Disclosure-Independent Governance", href: "/disclosure-independent-governance/" },
    { name: "Power User Trap℠", href: "/frameworks/power-user-trap/" },
  ],
});

const railItems = [
  { label: "Framework", value: "Power User Trap℠" },
  { label: "Defined by", value: "Lozen Advisory" },
  { label: "Category", value: "Disclosure-Independent Governance" },
  { label: "Primary signal", value: "Hidden verification, correction, and system-repair labor" },
  { label: "Primary applications", value: "AI adoption, workforce risk, board evidence, human capacity" },
];

const railLinks = [
  {
    label: "Disclosure-Independent Governance",
    href: "/disclosure-independent-governance/",
    description: "The canonical Lozen Advisory category hub for risks legacy systems were not built to see.",
  },
  {
    label: "The Name Standard℠",
    href: "/blog/the-name-standard-ai-governance/",
    description: "Lozen Advisory’s human-attribution standard for AI-assisted decisions and sign-off.",
  },
  {
    label: "AI Legislation Tracker",
    href: "/ai-legislation-tracker/",
    description: "AI legislation intelligence for human accountability and board evidence.",
  },
  {
    label: "AI Workforce Materiality",
    href: "/ai-workforce-materiality/",
    description: "Lozen Advisory analysis on AI adoption, human capacity, and workforce materiality.",
  },
];
---

<NarrativePage
  title={pageTitle}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  metaDescription={description}
  canonical={canonicalUrl}
  eyebrow="Lozen Advisory Framework"
  typeLabel="Framework Page"
  dateLabel="Updated July 2, 2026"
  textHeroLayout="wide"
  articleJsonLd={articleJsonLd}
  railTitle="Framework Context"
  railItems={railItems}
  railLinks={railLinks}
  bottomCta={{
    label: "Next step",
    title: "Evaluate whether AI adoption is creating governed capacity or hidden review burden.",
    description:
      "Lozen Advisory’s Governance Readiness Briefing helps leadership teams identify where AI-assisted work is relying on human review without the authority, capacity, evidence, or refusal rights required for accountable sign-off.",
    href: "/request-briefing/",
    linkLabel: "Request Briefing →",
  }}
  relatedEyebrow="Related Architecture"
  relatedTitle="Continue through Disclosure-Independent Governance"
  relatedItems={[
    {
      label: "Disclosure-Independent Governance",
      href: "/disclosure-independent-governance/",
      description: "The Lozen Advisory category hub for risks legacy systems were not built to see.",
    },
    {
      label: "The Name Standard℠",
      href: "/blog/the-name-standard-ai-governance/",
      description: "Lozen Advisory’s human-attribution standard for AI-assisted decisions and sign-off.",
    },
    {
      label: "Mandatory AI Use Is Not AI Governance",
      href: "/blog/mandatory-ai-use-governance/",
      description: "Why AI mandates can relocate accountability without building governance capacity.",
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    <strong>Lozen Advisory’s Power User Trap℠</strong> is a framework for identifying when organizations relocate hidden verification, correction, complexity, or system-repair work onto high-capacity employees without formally assigning the authority, capacity, evidence, or refusal rights required to carry that work responsibly.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Power User Trap℠ is not simply a workload problem. It is a governance problem. It appears when the organization reads continued performance as proof that a system is working, while the real operating condition depends on unmeasured human capacity.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the Power User Trap℠ matters now</h2>

  <p>
    AI adoption intensifies the Power User Trap℠ because the people most capable of detecting system failure often become the people expected to correct it. They check outputs, catch hallucinations, repair workflows, train colleagues, interpret ambiguity, and prevent errors from leaving the organization.
  </p>

  <p>
    If that work is not formally assigned, measured, staffed, documented, or governed, leadership may believe AI has created efficiency while the organization has actually relocated risk into hidden human review work.
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>
      AI adoption can create the appearance of governed capacity while moving accountability into the people least likely to let the system fail.
    </p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>How the trap forms</h2>

  <p>
    The Power User Trap℠ usually forms through ordinary operational assumptions rather than a formal decision.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>A new AI tool is deployed before review work is properly scoped.</li>
    <li>The most capable employees become informal validators, fixers, or escalation points.</li>
    <li>Corrections are treated as part of normal performance rather than as governance evidence.</li>
    <li>AI-related friction is absorbed before it becomes visible in productivity, quality, or risk data.</li>
    <li>Leadership sees output continue and assumes the system is stable.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The organization may have an AI policy, a tool inventory, or a review process. The Power User Trap℠ asks whether those controls can see the human burden that keeps the system functioning.
  </p>

  <h2>Where it appears</h2>

  <p>
    In AI governance, the Power User Trap℠ appears when employees quietly verify, rewrite, correct, escalate, or rescue AI-assisted work without the authority or documentation needed to make accountability real.
  </p>

  <p>
    In workforce systems, it appears when high-capacity people keep absorbing complexity, mentoring, institutional memory, emotional labor, or informal problem-solving while the organization interprets their continued performance as stability.
  </p>

  <p>
    In both settings, the institution benefits from hidden capacity while failing to price, govern, or protect it.
  </p>

  <h2>The board-level question</h2>

  <p>
    The board-level question is not whether employees are using AI or whether humans are present in the workflow.
  </p>

  <p>
    The question is whether the organization can prove who is carrying verification, correction, and sign-off responsibility; whether that responsibility has been formally assigned; and whether the people carrying it have enough capacity, information, authority, documentation, and refusal rights to make accountability defensible.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is why Power User Trap℠ connects directly to <a href="/disclosure-independent-governance/">Disclosure-Independent Governance</a> and <a href="/blog/the-name-standard-ai-governance/">The Name Standard℠</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What leaders should ask</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Which employees are repeatedly asked to verify or correct AI-assisted output?</li>
    <li>Is that review work formally assigned, staffed, and documented?</li>
    <li>Do reviewers have authority to stop, escalate, or refuse AI-assisted work?</li>
    <li>Is the organization measuring AI-related correction labor as part of the cost of adoption?</li>
    <li>Does continued output depend on a small group of people preventing failure from becoming visible?</li>
    <li>Can leadership distinguish AI-enabled capacity from human capacity being quietly overused?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How Lozen Advisory uses the framework</h2>

  <p>
    Lozen Advisory uses the Power User Trap℠ framework to help leadership teams evaluate whether AI adoption is creating governed capacity or merely relocating accountability into human review work.
  </p>

  <p>
    The goal is not to slow AI adoption. The goal is to prevent organizations from mistaking hidden human repair work for system maturity.
  </p>
</NarrativePage>
